1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,152
There is a hole in your airplane

2
00:00:02,152 --> 00:00:02,623
window.

3
00:00:02,803 --> 00:00:04,949
There are tiny fins on the wing,

4
00:00:04,949 --> 00:00:06,692
little sticks on the tail,

5
00:00:06,692 --> 00:00:09,038
and a spiral painted on the engine.

6
00:00:09,218 --> 00:00:11,542
There is an ashtray in a bathroom

7
00:00:11,542 --> 00:00:14,500
where smoking has been banned for decades.

8
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:16,155
You have seen all of them.

9
00:00:16,335 --> 00:00:18,436
Almost nobody can explain them.

10
00:00:18,616 --> 00:00:21,101
Today we are going to explain twelve

11
00:00:21,101 --> 00:00:23,241
of them, and one of these parts

12
00:00:23,241 --> 00:00:25,726
was the reason a whole generation of

13
00:00:25,726 --> 00:00:27,452
jets fell out of the sky.

14
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:30,110
Every part on an airliner has to

15
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:31,192
earn its place.

16
00:00:31,372 --> 00:00:32,366
It adds weight,

17
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:33,228
it adds cost,

18
00:00:33,228 --> 00:00:35,017
and it has to be inspected.

19
00:00:35,197 --> 00:00:37,480
So when something looks strange,

20
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:39,263
pointless, or even silly,

21
00:00:39,263 --> 00:00:41,903
there is almost always a story behind

22
00:00:41,903 --> 00:00:42,117
it.

23
00:00:42,297 --> 00:00:44,328
Sometimes it is physics.

24
00:00:44,508 --> 00:00:46,238
Sometimes it is money.

25
00:00:46,418 --> 00:00:48,699
And sometimes it is a lesson that

26
00:00:48,699 --> 00:00:50,150
was written in blood,

27
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:52,431
and turned into a rule that every

28
00:00:52,431 --> 00:00:54,642
airplane in the world now has to

29
00:00:54,642 --> 00:00:55,126
follow.

30
00:00:55,476 --> 00:00:57,205
Here is how this works.

31
00:00:57,385 --> 00:00:58,452
For every part,

32
00:00:58,452 --> 00:01:00,658
we start with a picture and one

33
00:01:00,658 --> 00:01:01,298
question.

34
00:01:01,478 --> 00:01:03,010
Why does this exist?

35
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:05,342
Then we give you the real answer,

36
00:01:05,342 --> 00:01:06,386
with the source,

37
00:01:06,386 --> 00:01:07,951
and the story behind it.

38
00:01:08,131 --> 00:01:11,126
Every number in this video comes from

39
00:01:11,126 --> 00:01:12,340
a named source,

40
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:16,145
like NASA, the Federal Aviation Administration,

41
00:01:16,145 --> 00:01:19,221
or an official accident investigation,

42
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:21,892
and every source is listed in the

43
00:01:21,892 --> 00:01:22,864
description.

44
00:01:23,044 --> 00:01:24,803
Let's start with the one you can

45
00:01:24,803 --> 00:01:25,133
touch.

46
00:01:25,483 --> 00:01:27,286
Why is there a tiny hole in

47
00:01:27,286 --> 00:01:28,688
your airplane window?

48
00:01:28,868 --> 00:01:30,311
It looks like a mistake,

49
00:01:30,311 --> 00:01:32,537
but it is keeping the window working.

50
00:01:32,717 --> 00:01:35,515
An airplane window is not one pane.

51
00:01:35,695 --> 00:01:36,763
It is three.

52
00:01:36,943 --> 00:01:39,209
The inner one is just a scratch

53
00:01:39,209 --> 00:01:39,648
guard.

54
00:01:39,828 --> 00:01:42,025
The middle and outer panes are the

55
00:01:42,025 --> 00:01:42,800
real window.

56
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:44,096
The tiny hole,

57
00:01:44,096 --> 00:01:47,044
called a breather hole or bleed hole,

58
00:01:47,044 --> 00:01:48,797
is in the middle pane.

59
00:01:48,977 --> 00:01:51,521
It lets the air pressure between the

60
00:01:51,521 --> 00:01:53,075
panes match the cabin,

61
00:01:53,075 --> 00:01:54,559
so during the flight,

62
00:01:54,559 --> 00:01:57,174
the cabin pressure pushes only on the

63
00:01:57,174 --> 00:01:57,951
outer pane.

64
00:01:58,131 --> 00:02:00,790
The middle pane carries almost nothing.

65
00:02:00,970 --> 00:02:02,111
It is a spare,

66
00:02:02,111 --> 00:02:04,881
waiting in case the outer one ever

67
00:02:04,881 --> 00:02:05,370
fails.

68
00:02:05,719 --> 00:02:07,310
Think about what that means.

69
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:09,780
At cruise, the air outside is thin

70
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:12,338
and cold, and the cabin is pressurized

71
00:02:12,338 --> 00:02:14,561
so that you can breathe normally.

72
00:02:14,741 --> 00:02:17,827
That difference in pressure pushes outward on

73
00:02:17,827 --> 00:02:19,679
every window, every flight,

74
00:02:19,679 --> 00:02:22,080
thousands of times over the life of

75
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:22,972
the airplane.

76
00:02:23,152 --> 00:02:25,810
By loading only one pane and keeping

77
00:02:25,810 --> 00:02:27,582
a second one in reserve,

78
00:02:27,582 --> 00:02:30,241
the designers built a backup that is

79
00:02:30,241 --> 00:02:32,382
always there and never tired.

80
00:02:32,562 --> 00:02:34,309
If the outer pane cracks,

81
00:02:34,309 --> 00:02:36,196
the middle pane takes over.

82
00:02:36,545 --> 00:02:38,217
And the hole has a second job.

83
00:02:38,397 --> 00:02:40,856
Air between two panes of glass can

84
00:02:40,856 --> 00:02:44,254
hold moisture, and when the outside temperature

85
00:02:44,254 --> 00:02:46,062
drops far below freezing,

86
00:02:46,062 --> 00:02:48,665
that moisture would fog or frost the

87
00:02:48,665 --> 00:02:49,171
window.

88
00:02:49,351 --> 00:02:52,047
The breather hole lets that moist air

89
00:02:52,047 --> 00:02:53,285
move and dry out,

90
00:02:53,285 --> 00:02:55,180
so the window stays clear.

91
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,893
One tiny hole,

92
00:02:56,893 --> 00:02:57,879
two jobs.

93
00:02:58,059 --> 00:03:00,684
A spare pane that is never stressed,

94
00:03:00,684 --> 00:03:02,726
and a clear view of the sky.

95
00:03:03,077 --> 00:03:05,879
Why are airplane windows always rounded,

96
00:03:05,879 --> 00:03:07,070
and never square?

97
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:09,864
The answer starts with the first jet

98
00:03:09,864 --> 00:03:11,316
airliner in history,

99
00:03:11,316 --> 00:03:12,986
the de Havilland Comet.

100
00:03:13,166 --> 00:03:16,614
It entered passenger service on the second

101
00:03:16,614 --> 00:03:18,831
of May, nineteen fifty-two.

102
00:03:19,011 --> 00:03:20,997
Less than two years later,

103
00:03:20,997 --> 00:03:22,830
on the tenth of January,

104
00:03:22,830 --> 00:03:26,114
nineteen fifty-four, a Comet broke apart in

105
00:03:26,114 --> 00:03:28,558
the air near the island of Elba,

106
00:03:28,558 --> 00:03:31,689
and all thirty-five people on board died.

107
00:03:31,869 --> 00:03:33,333
Three months later,

108
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:36,029
another Comet broke up near Naples,

109
00:03:36,029 --> 00:03:39,033
killing all twenty-one people on board.

110
00:03:39,213 --> 00:03:41,115
The cause was metal fatigue,

111
00:03:41,115 --> 00:03:43,358
and it started at a sharp corner.

112
00:03:43,708 --> 00:03:46,703
Engineers put a Comet fuselage in a

113
00:03:46,703 --> 00:03:50,213
giant water tank and pressurized it again

114
00:03:50,213 --> 00:03:53,808
and again, simulating flight after flight.

115
00:03:53,988 --> 00:03:57,264
After three thousand and fifty-seven cycles,

116
00:03:57,264 --> 00:03:59,050
the fuselage burst open.

117
00:03:59,230 --> 00:04:01,075
On the crashed airplane,

118
00:04:01,075 --> 00:04:04,150
investigators traced the crack to a tiny

119
00:04:04,150 --> 00:04:06,379
rivet hole at the corner of a

120
00:04:06,379 --> 00:04:08,378
square cutout in the roof,

121
00:04:08,378 --> 00:04:10,991
a window for a navigation antenna.

122
00:04:11,171 --> 00:04:14,083
Every flight, the pressure flexed that corner

123
00:04:14,083 --> 00:04:14,666
a little.

124
00:04:14,846 --> 00:04:17,214
Eventually, the metal simply gave up.

125
00:04:17,564 --> 00:04:19,514
Now here is the twist.

126
00:04:19,694 --> 00:04:22,477
Most people say the square passenger windows

127
00:04:22,477 --> 00:04:23,932
brought the Comet down.

128
00:04:24,112 --> 00:04:25,598
That is not quite true.

129
00:04:25,778 --> 00:04:28,088
According to the investigation,

130
00:04:28,088 --> 00:04:30,771
the crack began at that roof cutout,

131
00:04:30,771 --> 00:04:32,709
not at a passenger window.

132
00:04:32,889 --> 00:04:34,744
But the lesson was the same for

133
00:04:34,744 --> 00:04:35,582
every opening.

134
00:04:35,762 --> 00:04:38,758
A sharp corner concentrates stress in one

135
00:04:38,758 --> 00:04:41,461
point, and a rounded shape spreads it

136
00:04:41,461 --> 00:04:41,753
out.

137
00:04:41,933 --> 00:04:42,819
That is why,

138
00:04:42,819 --> 00:04:45,106
on every pressurized jet today,

139
00:04:45,106 --> 00:04:47,984
every window and every door has rounded

140
00:04:47,984 --> 00:04:48,574
corners.

141
00:04:48,924 --> 00:04:51,158
Why are there tiny fins sticking out

142
00:04:51,158 --> 00:04:51,965
of this wing?

143
00:04:52,145 --> 00:04:54,185
They look like a factory forgot to

144
00:04:54,185 --> 00:04:55,025
sand them off.

145
00:04:55,205 --> 00:04:57,714
They are called vortex generators,

146
00:04:57,714 --> 00:05:00,150
and each one is a miniature wing.

147
00:05:00,330 --> 00:05:01,889
As the air passes,

148
00:05:01,889 --> 00:05:04,659
each fin spins off a small swirl

149
00:05:04,659 --> 00:05:05,265
of air.

150
00:05:05,445 --> 00:05:08,295
That swirl grabs fast moving air from

151
00:05:08,295 --> 00:05:10,837
above and mixes it into the slow,

152
00:05:10,837 --> 00:05:13,611
tired layer of air right against the

153
00:05:13,611 --> 00:05:14,535
wing's skin.

154
00:05:14,715 --> 00:05:17,454
That energized layer stays attached to the

155
00:05:17,454 --> 00:05:18,498
wing for longer,

156
00:05:18,498 --> 00:05:20,585
so the wing keeps making lift at

157
00:05:20,585 --> 00:05:23,063
angles where it would otherwise stall.

158
00:05:23,243 --> 00:05:25,297
A row of fins the size of

159
00:05:25,297 --> 00:05:28,502
your thumb, buying safety at low speed.

160
00:05:28,853 --> 00:05:31,186
The thin layer of air touching the

161
00:05:31,186 --> 00:05:33,520
wing is called the boundary layer.

162
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:36,016
It is slowed down by friction with

163
00:05:36,016 --> 00:05:38,263
the skin, and when the wing tilts

164
00:05:38,263 --> 00:05:41,464
up, it struggles against rising pressure toward

165
00:05:41,464 --> 00:05:42,895
the back of the wing.

166
00:05:43,075 --> 00:05:44,789
If it slows too much,

167
00:05:44,789 --> 00:05:48,054
it separates, and the smooth flow breaks

168
00:05:48,054 --> 00:05:48,462
down.

169
00:05:48,642 --> 00:05:49,629
That is a stall.

170
00:05:49,809 --> 00:05:53,588
Vortex generators fight that separation by constantly

171
00:05:53,588 --> 00:05:56,368
feeding energy into the boundary layer.

172
00:05:56,719 --> 00:05:59,349
On airliners, you often find them right

173
00:05:59,349 --> 00:06:00,833
in front of the flaps,

174
00:06:00,833 --> 00:06:02,182
or near the engines,

175
00:06:02,182 --> 00:06:04,745
because those are the places where the

176
00:06:04,745 --> 00:06:06,633
airflow has the hardest job.

177
00:06:06,813 --> 00:06:08,363
On high speed jets,

178
00:06:08,363 --> 00:06:11,951
they also help keep control surfaces working

179
00:06:11,951 --> 00:06:13,501
behind shock waves.

180
00:06:13,681 --> 00:06:15,933
Small airplanes use them too.

181
00:06:16,113 --> 00:06:18,562
Kits with dozens of tiny fins are

182
00:06:18,562 --> 00:06:21,086
sold to let bush planes fly slower

183
00:06:21,086 --> 00:06:22,348
and land shorter.

184
00:06:22,528 --> 00:06:25,016
It is one of the cheapest upgrades

185
00:06:25,016 --> 00:06:25,895
in aviation.

186
00:06:26,244 --> 00:06:28,348
Why are there little sticks hanging off

187
00:06:28,348 --> 00:06:30,398
the back of every airliner's wings and

188
00:06:30,398 --> 00:06:30,668
tail?

189
00:06:30,848 --> 00:06:32,258
They are not antennas,

190
00:06:32,258 --> 00:06:34,052
and they are not decoration.

191
00:06:34,232 --> 00:06:36,213
They are static wicks,

192
00:06:36,213 --> 00:06:39,004
also called static dischargers.

193
00:06:39,184 --> 00:06:41,664
When a jet flies through rain,

194
00:06:41,664 --> 00:06:43,896
snow, ice crystals or dust,

195
00:06:43,896 --> 00:06:47,782
friction charges the whole airplane with static

196
00:06:47,782 --> 00:06:48,774
electricity.

197
00:06:48,954 --> 00:06:51,811
More than five hundred thousand volts have

198
00:06:51,811 --> 00:06:54,736
been measured on small airplanes in flight.

199
00:06:54,916 --> 00:06:58,271
If that charge leaks off in uncontrolled

200
00:06:58,271 --> 00:07:01,123
sparks, it creates a roar of radio

201
00:07:01,123 --> 00:07:01,626
noise.

202
00:07:01,806 --> 00:07:04,154
The wicks bleed it off quietly,

203
00:07:04,154 --> 00:07:05,744
through sharp points,

204
00:07:05,744 --> 00:07:08,167
so the pilots can still hear the

205
00:07:08,167 --> 00:07:08,621
radio.

206
00:07:08,971 --> 00:07:12,385
Pilots call this problem precipitation static.

207
00:07:12,565 --> 00:07:16,310
Without dischargers, the charge escapes from the

208
00:07:16,310 --> 00:07:18,573
wing tips and tail edges in a

209
00:07:18,573 --> 00:07:20,680
crackling corona discharge.

210
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:21,888
In the headset,

211
00:07:21,888 --> 00:07:23,739
it sounds like a loud hiss.

212
00:07:23,919 --> 00:07:25,940
It can drown out voice radio,

213
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:27,960
confuse navigation receivers,

214
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:31,443
and even make some instruments behave erratically.

215
00:07:31,623 --> 00:07:33,520
In the middle of a storm,

216
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,024
that is exactly when you need the

217
00:07:36,024 --> 00:07:36,859
radio most.

218
00:07:37,208 --> 00:07:39,623
Static wicks have a long history.

219
00:07:39,803 --> 00:07:42,986
An early patent for a static discharger

220
00:07:42,986 --> 00:07:45,353
was filed in nineteen twenty.

221
00:07:45,533 --> 00:07:48,775
Modern carbon wicks were patented in the

222
00:07:48,775 --> 00:07:51,613
early nineteen fifties by a team of

223
00:07:51,613 --> 00:07:54,693
engineers that included Mary Sullivan.

224
00:07:54,873 --> 00:07:56,580
Today, they are simple,

225
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:57,842
light, and cheap,

226
00:07:57,842 --> 00:08:00,587
and they are on almost every airliner

227
00:08:00,587 --> 00:08:02,071
you will ever board.

228
00:08:02,251 --> 00:08:03,971
Next time you look out at the

229
00:08:03,971 --> 00:08:04,980
wing, count them.

230
00:08:05,329 --> 00:08:07,064
Why does the tip of this wing

231
00:08:07,064 --> 00:08:08,081
bend straight up?

232
00:08:08,261 --> 00:08:10,050
It is called a winglet,

233
00:08:10,050 --> 00:08:12,150
and it exists to save fuel.

234
00:08:12,330 --> 00:08:14,982
A wing works because the pressure under

235
00:08:14,982 --> 00:08:17,225
it is higher than the pressure on

236
00:08:17,225 --> 00:08:17,497
top.

237
00:08:17,677 --> 00:08:18,806
But at the tip,

238
00:08:18,806 --> 00:08:21,665
that high pressure air tries to escape

239
00:08:21,665 --> 00:08:22,869
around the edge,

240
00:08:22,869 --> 00:08:25,202
and it rolls up into a spinning

241
00:08:25,202 --> 00:08:28,137
tornado of air called a wingtip vortex.

242
00:08:28,317 --> 00:08:30,692
That vortex costs energy,

243
00:08:30,692 --> 00:08:33,542
a penalty called induced drag.

244
00:08:33,722 --> 00:08:36,404
A winglet weakens that vortex.

245
00:08:36,584 --> 00:08:38,172
According to NASA,

246
00:08:38,172 --> 00:08:42,231
winglet technology generally saves four to six

247
00:08:42,231 --> 00:08:43,643
percent of fuel.

248
00:08:43,823 --> 00:08:45,064
On a big airline,

249
00:08:45,064 --> 00:08:47,108
that is millions of dollars.

250
00:08:47,458 --> 00:08:49,815
The modern winglet came from NASA.

251
00:08:49,995 --> 00:08:51,898
In nineteen seventy-six,

252
00:08:51,898 --> 00:08:53,564
after the oil crisis,

253
00:08:53,564 --> 00:08:58,322
NASA engineer Richard Whitcomb published research predicting

254
00:08:58,322 --> 00:09:01,414
that winglets could cut induced drag by

255
00:09:01,414 --> 00:09:03,080
about twenty percent.

256
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:05,801
NASA, the US Air Force and Boeing

257
00:09:05,801 --> 00:09:08,189
then flight tested them on a KC

258
00:09:08,189 --> 00:09:12,117
one thirty-five tanker in nineteen seventy-nine and

259
00:09:12,117 --> 00:09:13,349
nineteen eighty.

260
00:09:13,529 --> 00:09:15,723
The results matched the prediction.

261
00:09:16,073 --> 00:09:18,198
Then the numbers became enormous.

262
00:09:18,378 --> 00:09:19,752
According to NASA,

263
00:09:19,752 --> 00:09:23,873
a typical Southwest Airlines Boeing seven thirty-seven

264
00:09:23,873 --> 00:09:27,460
seven hundred with blended winglets saves about

265
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:30,512
one hundred thousand gallons of fuel per

266
00:09:30,512 --> 00:09:30,894
year.

267
00:09:31,074 --> 00:09:32,495
And by twenty ten,

268
00:09:32,495 --> 00:09:36,129
blended winglets had saved roughly two billion

269
00:09:36,129 --> 00:09:38,498
gallons of jet fuel worldwide,

270
00:09:38,498 --> 00:09:41,105
worth about four billion dollars.

271
00:09:41,285 --> 00:09:42,978
A bent piece of wing,

272
00:09:42,978 --> 00:09:45,801
paying for itself every single day.

273
00:09:46,150 --> 00:09:47,894
Why are there long pods,

274
00:09:47,894 --> 00:09:49,274
shaped like canoes,

275
00:09:49,274 --> 00:09:50,945
hanging under the wing?

276
00:09:51,125 --> 00:09:53,449
They are called flap track fairings,

277
00:09:53,449 --> 00:09:54,933
and they have two jobs.

278
00:09:55,113 --> 00:09:57,151
The first is obvious once you know

279
00:09:57,151 --> 00:09:57,331
it.

280
00:09:57,511 --> 00:10:00,523
Inside each canoe is the heavy mechanism

281
00:10:00,523 --> 00:10:03,459
that slides the flaps backward and down

282
00:10:03,459 --> 00:10:05,266
for takeoff and landing.

283
00:10:05,446 --> 00:10:07,339
The second job is hidden.

284
00:10:07,519 --> 00:10:09,938
Their long shape sticks out past the

285
00:10:09,938 --> 00:10:11,819
back of the wing on purpose,

286
00:10:11,819 --> 00:10:14,372
and it actually reduces drag at cruise

287
00:10:14,372 --> 00:10:14,775
speed.

288
00:10:14,955 --> 00:10:16,383
They are not just covers.

289
00:10:16,563 --> 00:10:18,463
They are shaped to make the whole

290
00:10:18,463 --> 00:10:20,882
airplane slip through the air more easily.

291
00:10:21,231 --> 00:10:23,982
That shape comes from a famous rule

292
00:10:23,982 --> 00:10:26,419
in aerodynamics, the area rule,

293
00:10:26,419 --> 00:10:29,799
developed by NASA's Richard Whitcomb in the

294
00:10:29,799 --> 00:10:31,135
nineteen fifties.

295
00:10:31,315 --> 00:10:33,207
Near the speed of sound,

296
00:10:33,207 --> 00:10:34,705
drag rises sharply.

297
00:10:34,885 --> 00:10:37,192
But if the cross section of the

298
00:10:37,192 --> 00:10:40,467
airplane changes smoothly from nose to tail,

299
00:10:40,467 --> 00:10:42,029
without sudden jumps,

300
00:10:42,029 --> 00:10:43,592
that drag is reduced.

301
00:10:43,772 --> 00:10:47,066
Airliners cruise at around eighty to eighty-five

302
00:10:47,066 --> 00:10:49,125
percent of the speed of sound,

303
00:10:49,125 --> 00:10:51,321
which is right in the zone where

304
00:10:51,321 --> 00:10:52,213
this matters.

305
00:10:52,562 --> 00:10:55,453
Whitcomb even proposed pods on the back

306
00:10:55,453 --> 00:10:57,603
of the wing for this purpose,

307
00:10:57,603 --> 00:10:58,715
and in Britain,

308
00:10:58,715 --> 00:11:02,792
the aerodynamicist Dietrich Küchemann reached a similar

309
00:11:02,792 --> 00:11:03,162
idea.

310
00:11:03,342 --> 00:11:06,721
His pods became known as Küchemann carrots.

311
00:11:06,901 --> 00:11:08,851
So when you look at those canoes

312
00:11:08,851 --> 00:11:09,766
under the wing,

313
00:11:09,766 --> 00:11:11,716
you are looking at two things at

314
00:11:11,716 --> 00:11:12,021
once.

315
00:11:12,201 --> 00:11:14,096
A cover for heavy machinery,

316
00:11:14,096 --> 00:11:16,533
and a carefully shaped trick to beat

317
00:11:16,533 --> 00:11:18,563
the drag of near sonic flight.

318
00:11:18,914 --> 00:11:20,826
Why does the back of this jet

319
00:11:20,826 --> 00:11:22,013
engine have teeth?

320
00:11:22,193 --> 00:11:25,197
These sawtooth edges are called chevrons,

321
00:11:25,197 --> 00:11:27,395
and they exist for one reason.

322
00:11:27,575 --> 00:11:28,190
Noise.

323
00:11:28,370 --> 00:11:30,601
The hot, fast exhaust of a jet

324
00:11:30,601 --> 00:11:33,500
engine slams into the slower air around

325
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:36,400
it, and that violent mixing creates the

326
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:38,482
roar you hear on the ground.

327
00:11:38,662 --> 00:11:41,518
The chevrons make the two streams blend

328
00:11:41,518 --> 00:11:44,154
more gently, so the roar is smaller.

329
00:11:44,334 --> 00:11:45,659
According to NASA,

330
00:11:45,659 --> 00:11:48,088
on the Boeing seven eighty-seven,

331
00:11:48,088 --> 00:11:51,696
its industry partner Boeing reported a sixty-nine

332
00:11:51,696 --> 00:11:54,051
percent smaller noise footprint.

333
00:11:54,231 --> 00:11:56,639
That is why the Dreamliner sounds so

334
00:11:56,639 --> 00:11:57,308
different.

335
00:11:57,658 --> 00:12:00,853
NASA spent the nineteen nineties and early

336
00:12:00,853 --> 00:12:04,733
two thousands refining chevron shapes with computer

337
00:12:04,733 --> 00:12:08,385
simulations, then ground tests and flight tests.

338
00:12:08,565 --> 00:12:11,598
Those tests showed lower noise both on

339
00:12:11,598 --> 00:12:14,951
the ground and inside the passenger cabin.

340
00:12:15,131 --> 00:12:17,468
For people living near airports,

341
00:12:17,468 --> 00:12:20,608
a smaller noise footprint means fewer homes

342
00:12:20,608 --> 00:12:23,676
inside the loudest zone around the runway.

343
00:12:24,025 --> 00:12:27,107
The Boeing seven forty-seven eight also uses

344
00:12:27,107 --> 00:12:30,678
chevrons, and Boeing reported a twenty-nine percent

345
00:12:30,678 --> 00:12:32,849
smaller noise footprint for it.

346
00:12:33,029 --> 00:12:34,724
But chevrons are not free.

347
00:12:34,904 --> 00:12:38,253
Sticking teeth into the exhaust stream costs

348
00:12:38,253 --> 00:12:39,775
a little efficiency,

349
00:12:39,775 --> 00:12:42,971
and some newer engine designs reduce noise

350
00:12:42,971 --> 00:12:44,645
in other ways instead.

351
00:12:44,825 --> 00:12:48,122
So engineers keep weighing the same trade-off.

352
00:12:48,302 --> 00:12:49,968
A quieter neighborhood,

353
00:12:49,968 --> 00:12:51,924
against a little more fuel.

354
00:12:52,274 --> 00:12:54,497
Why is there a white spiral painted

355
00:12:54,497 --> 00:12:56,338
on the center of this engine?

356
00:12:56,518 --> 00:12:59,014
Many people think it scares birds away.

357
00:12:59,194 --> 00:13:02,050
Boeing engineers say it does not reduce

358
00:13:02,050 --> 00:13:03,002
bird strikes.

359
00:13:03,182 --> 00:13:05,799
Its real job is to protect people

360
00:13:05,799 --> 00:13:06,909
on the ground.

361
00:13:07,089 --> 00:13:10,007
A turning fan can look almost still,

362
00:13:10,007 --> 00:13:11,628
especially at night.

363
00:13:11,808 --> 00:13:14,283
The spiral turns into a flickering blur

364
00:13:14,283 --> 00:13:16,059
the moment the engine moves,

365
00:13:16,059 --> 00:13:17,962
so the ground crew can tell at

366
00:13:17,962 --> 00:13:19,738
a glance that it is running.

367
00:13:19,918 --> 00:13:21,122
And that matters,

368
00:13:21,122 --> 00:13:23,246
because the area in front of a

369
00:13:23,246 --> 00:13:25,583
running engine is one of the most

370
00:13:25,583 --> 00:13:27,778
dangerous places at an airport.

371
00:13:28,127 --> 00:13:29,172
How dangerous?

372
00:13:29,352 --> 00:13:31,629
For a Boeing seven thirty-seven,

373
00:13:31,629 --> 00:13:33,834
the hazard zone in front of and

374
00:13:33,834 --> 00:13:36,751
beside the engine extends about nine feet

375
00:13:36,751 --> 00:13:39,170
at idle, and fourteen feet or more

376
00:13:39,170 --> 00:13:39,952
above idle.

377
00:13:40,132 --> 00:13:43,448
Bigger jets, like the seven seventy-seven,

378
00:13:43,448 --> 00:13:45,264
have much bigger zones.

379
00:13:45,444 --> 00:13:47,718
A person who walks into that area

380
00:13:47,718 --> 00:13:49,786
can be pulled into the engine.

381
00:13:49,966 --> 00:13:51,655
The spiral is a simple,

382
00:13:51,655 --> 00:13:54,886
cheap warning sign painted where everyone is

383
00:13:54,886 --> 00:13:56,061
already looking.

384
00:13:56,411 --> 00:13:57,491
And the bird theory?

385
00:13:57,671 --> 00:13:59,598
It is not completely crazy.

386
00:13:59,778 --> 00:14:01,634
In nineteen eighty-six,

387
00:14:01,634 --> 00:14:05,346
the Japanese airline All Nippon Airways tested

388
00:14:05,346 --> 00:14:08,411
eye-like markings on its engines for a

389
00:14:08,411 --> 00:14:11,477
year and reported a small reduction in

390
00:14:11,477 --> 00:14:12,526
bird strikes.

391
00:14:12,706 --> 00:14:15,346
But other studies were inconclusive,

392
00:14:15,346 --> 00:14:18,719
and a Rolls-Royce representative noted that in

393
00:14:18,719 --> 00:14:21,432
flight, the engine spins far too fast

394
00:14:21,432 --> 00:14:23,559
for a bird to see any pattern

395
00:14:23,559 --> 00:14:24,072
at all.

396
00:14:24,252 --> 00:14:26,296
The spiral is for people.

397
00:14:26,645 --> 00:14:28,780
Why is this small tube on the

398
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:31,208
nose of an airliner so important?

399
00:14:31,388 --> 00:14:32,583
It is a pitot tube,

400
00:14:32,583 --> 00:14:34,909
and it measures how fast the airplane

401
00:14:34,909 --> 00:14:36,543
is moving through the air.

402
00:14:36,723 --> 00:14:38,325
Air rams into the opening,

403
00:14:38,325 --> 00:14:40,789
and the pressure tells the computers the

404
00:14:40,789 --> 00:14:41,344
airspeed.

405
00:14:41,524 --> 00:14:44,449
Without it, the pilots are flying blind

406
00:14:44,449 --> 00:14:47,149
on one of the most important numbers

407
00:14:47,149 --> 00:14:48,049
in aviation.

408
00:14:48,229 --> 00:14:50,013
That is why the tubes are heated,

409
00:14:50,013 --> 00:14:51,906
and why there are always several of

410
00:14:51,906 --> 00:14:52,176
them.

411
00:14:52,356 --> 00:14:53,493
On the ground,

412
00:14:53,493 --> 00:14:56,415
you will often see them covered with

413
00:14:56,415 --> 00:14:59,582
red tags that say remove before flight,

414
00:14:59,582 --> 00:15:02,667
because even an insect nest inside one

415
00:15:02,667 --> 00:15:03,803
can be deadly.

416
00:15:04,153 --> 00:15:06,510
The most famous pitot tube story is

417
00:15:06,510 --> 00:15:08,867
Air France flight four forty-seven.

418
00:15:09,047 --> 00:15:10,495
On the first of June,

419
00:15:10,495 --> 00:15:11,736
two thousand nine,

420
00:15:11,736 --> 00:15:14,218
an Airbus A three thirty flying from

421
00:15:14,218 --> 00:15:16,700
Rio de Janeiro to Paris was cruising

422
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:19,665
at about thirty-five thousand feet over the

423
00:15:19,665 --> 00:15:20,286
Atlantic.

424
00:15:20,466 --> 00:15:23,245
Ice crystals blocked the pitot tubes,

425
00:15:23,245 --> 00:15:26,700
and the airspeed readings became inconsistent.

426
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:28,891
The autopilot disconnected,

427
00:15:28,891 --> 00:15:30,827
and the crew had to fly by

428
00:15:30,827 --> 00:15:31,199
hand.

429
00:15:31,548 --> 00:15:33,487
The blockage was temporary.

430
00:15:33,667 --> 00:15:36,370
But according to the final report from

431
00:15:36,370 --> 00:15:38,646
the French investigation agency,

432
00:15:38,646 --> 00:15:40,993
the BEA, the crew lost control of

433
00:15:40,993 --> 00:15:42,131
the flight path,

434
00:15:42,131 --> 00:15:44,620
and the airplane entered a stall it

435
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:46,113
never recovered from.

436
00:15:46,293 --> 00:15:47,967
It fell for about three and a

437
00:15:47,967 --> 00:15:50,159
half minutes before hitting the ocean.

438
00:15:50,339 --> 00:15:53,415
All two hundred twenty-eight people on board

439
00:15:53,415 --> 00:15:53,764
died.

440
00:15:53,944 --> 00:15:55,374
After the accident,

441
00:15:55,374 --> 00:15:59,136
European regulators ordered the older pitot probes

442
00:15:59,136 --> 00:16:00,942
replaced on Airbus jets,

443
00:16:00,942 --> 00:16:04,705
and pilot training for unreliable airspeed changed

444
00:16:04,705 --> 00:16:05,984
around the world.

445
00:16:06,333 --> 00:16:08,984
Why does a modern jet airliner carry

446
00:16:08,984 --> 00:16:11,709
a tiny propeller hidden in its belly?

447
00:16:11,889 --> 00:16:14,379
It is called a ram air turbine,

448
00:16:14,379 --> 00:16:16,467
or RAT, and it is the last

449
00:16:16,467 --> 00:16:17,752
line of defense.

450
00:16:17,932 --> 00:16:21,071
If an airliner loses all its engine

451
00:16:21,071 --> 00:16:24,031
power, the RAT drops out into the

452
00:16:24,031 --> 00:16:24,480
wind.

453
00:16:24,660 --> 00:16:27,144
The air spins it like a windmill,

454
00:16:27,144 --> 00:16:29,477
and it drives a hydraulic pump,

455
00:16:29,477 --> 00:16:31,057
a generator, or both.

456
00:16:31,237 --> 00:16:33,218
That powers the essentials,

457
00:16:33,218 --> 00:16:36,520
the flight controls and the core instruments,

458
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:38,575
so the pilots can still fly.

459
00:16:38,755 --> 00:16:41,243
It works without electricity to deploy,

460
00:16:41,243 --> 00:16:43,412
because it is built for the moment

461
00:16:43,412 --> 00:16:45,454
when everything else has failed.

462
00:16:45,803 --> 00:16:47,338
In two thousand one,

463
00:16:47,338 --> 00:16:49,946
Air Transat flight two thirty-six,

464
00:16:49,946 --> 00:16:52,554
an Airbus A three thirty with more

465
00:16:52,554 --> 00:16:55,240
than three hundred people on board,

466
00:16:55,240 --> 00:16:58,538
lost both engines over the Atlantic because

467
00:16:58,538 --> 00:16:59,689
of a fuel leak.

468
00:16:59,869 --> 00:17:01,622
The RAT deployed.

469
00:17:01,802 --> 00:17:04,754
Its pump kept the flight controls alive,

470
00:17:04,754 --> 00:17:07,485
and the airplane glided for more than

471
00:17:07,485 --> 00:17:11,323
fifteen minutes, covering roughly one hundred twenty

472
00:17:11,323 --> 00:17:14,202
kilometers, to a landing in the Azores.

473
00:17:14,382 --> 00:17:15,879
Everyone survived.

474
00:17:16,229 --> 00:17:18,067
And in two thousand nine,

475
00:17:18,067 --> 00:17:21,447
when US Airways flight fifteen forty-nine lost

476
00:17:21,447 --> 00:17:24,459
both engines after hitting birds over New

477
00:17:24,459 --> 00:17:27,105
York, the RAT deployed on the Airbus

478
00:17:27,105 --> 00:17:29,677
A three twenty and helped power its

479
00:17:29,677 --> 00:17:32,616
fly by wire controls during the ditching

480
00:17:32,616 --> 00:17:34,086
in the Hudson River.

481
00:17:34,266 --> 00:17:35,961
All on board survived.

482
00:17:36,141 --> 00:17:39,136
Most passengers will never see a RAT.

483
00:17:39,316 --> 00:17:40,569
On most flights,

484
00:17:40,569 --> 00:17:42,214
it stays folded away,

485
00:17:42,214 --> 00:17:44,250
waiting for the worst day.

486
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:47,006
Why is there a small triangle sticker

487
00:17:47,006 --> 00:17:48,827
above some airplane windows?

488
00:17:49,007 --> 00:17:50,983
It marks the window with the best

489
00:17:50,983 --> 00:17:52,002
view of the wing.

490
00:17:52,182 --> 00:17:54,669
If the crew needs to check the

491
00:17:54,669 --> 00:17:56,078
flaps, the slats,

492
00:17:56,078 --> 00:17:58,649
the engine, or ice on the wing,

493
00:17:58,649 --> 00:18:01,799
they know exactly which window to walk

494
00:18:01,799 --> 00:18:04,535
to, without searching row by row.

495
00:18:04,715 --> 00:18:06,809
There is usually at least one on

496
00:18:06,809 --> 00:18:08,314
each side of the cabin.

497
00:18:08,494 --> 00:18:10,455
You are not expected to do anything

498
00:18:10,455 --> 00:18:11,408
if you sit there.

499
00:18:11,588 --> 00:18:13,512
But if you want the best photo

500
00:18:13,512 --> 00:18:14,282
of the wing,

501
00:18:14,282 --> 00:18:15,821
or to watch the deicing,

502
00:18:15,821 --> 00:18:16,975
that is your seat.

503
00:18:17,325 --> 00:18:19,169
A former airline pilot,

504
00:18:19,169 --> 00:18:22,537
Daniel Bubb, told Reader's Digest that the

505
00:18:22,537 --> 00:18:25,102
main purpose is to help the crew

506
00:18:25,102 --> 00:18:28,469
locate problems quickly in an emergency or

507
00:18:28,469 --> 00:18:31,035
a malfunction of the wing flaps,

508
00:18:31,035 --> 00:18:33,440
slats, engine and other parts.

509
00:18:33,620 --> 00:18:36,332
The triangles are more common on larger

510
00:18:36,332 --> 00:18:37,932
Airbus and Boeing jets,

511
00:18:37,932 --> 00:18:40,505
and some airlines skip them entirely.

512
00:18:40,685 --> 00:18:43,558
Fans have even nicknamed it the William

513
00:18:43,558 --> 00:18:46,652
Shatner seat, after a famous Twilight Zone

514
00:18:46,652 --> 00:18:49,230
episode with a monster on the wing.

515
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:51,593
Why is there an ashtray in an

516
00:18:51,593 --> 00:18:54,856
airplane bathroom, when smoking has been banned

517
00:18:54,856 --> 00:18:56,453
on flights for decades?

518
00:18:56,633 --> 00:18:58,537
Because the law requires it.

519
00:18:58,717 --> 00:19:02,514
US federal regulations say that lavatories must

520
00:19:02,514 --> 00:19:05,745
have self-contained, removable ashtrays,

521
00:19:05,745 --> 00:19:08,492
even though smoking is prohibited.

522
00:19:08,672 --> 00:19:10,228
The logic is simple.

523
00:19:10,408 --> 00:19:12,324
Some people break the rules.

524
00:19:12,504 --> 00:19:15,047
If someone lights a cigarette anyway,

525
00:19:15,047 --> 00:19:17,109
the worst thing they can do is

526
00:19:17,109 --> 00:19:19,171
throw it in the trash bin full

527
00:19:19,171 --> 00:19:20,271
of paper towels.

528
00:19:20,451 --> 00:19:22,640
The ashtray gives them a safe place

529
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:23,516
to put it out.

530
00:19:23,696 --> 00:19:25,799
It is a part designed for people

531
00:19:25,799 --> 00:19:27,573
who are breaking the rules.

532
00:19:27,923 --> 00:19:30,905
The rule is written in Title fourteen

533
00:19:30,905 --> 00:19:33,724
of the Code of Federal Regulations,

534
00:19:33,724 --> 00:19:37,270
part twenty-five, section eight fifty-three.

535
00:19:37,450 --> 00:19:40,287
Smoking was banned on all US domestic

536
00:19:40,287 --> 00:19:43,048
flights in February nineteen ninety,

537
00:19:43,048 --> 00:19:45,808
except a few flights over six hours,

538
00:19:45,808 --> 00:19:47,572
and since two thousand,

539
00:19:47,572 --> 00:19:50,103
it has been banned on all flights

540
00:19:50,103 --> 00:19:52,403
to and from the United States.

541
00:19:52,583 --> 00:19:54,408
Yet the ashtrays remain,

542
00:19:54,408 --> 00:19:56,462
and if more than half of an

543
00:19:56,462 --> 00:19:59,504
airplane's lavatory ashtrays are broken,

544
00:19:59,504 --> 00:20:03,079
they must be repaired within seventy-two hours.

545
00:20:03,428 --> 00:20:05,031
So here are the twelve.

546
00:20:05,211 --> 00:20:07,528
A hole that keeps a spare window

547
00:20:07,528 --> 00:20:07,962
ready.

548
00:20:08,142 --> 00:20:11,010
Rounded corners born from a disaster.

549
00:20:11,190 --> 00:20:13,041
Fins that delay a stall,

550
00:20:13,041 --> 00:20:15,355
sticks that silence the radio,

551
00:20:15,355 --> 00:20:18,595
and wingtips that save billions of gallons

552
00:20:18,595 --> 00:20:19,212
of fuel.

553
00:20:19,392 --> 00:20:21,060
Canoes that beat drag,

554
00:20:21,060 --> 00:20:22,879
teeth that quiet a city,

555
00:20:22,879 --> 00:20:25,457
and a spiral that saves the ground

556
00:20:25,457 --> 00:20:25,836
crew.

557
00:20:26,016 --> 00:20:28,827
A tube that cost two hundred twenty-eight

558
00:20:28,827 --> 00:20:30,198
lives when it froze,

559
00:20:30,198 --> 00:20:32,255
a propeller for the worst day,

560
00:20:32,255 --> 00:20:33,901
a triangle for the crew,

561
00:20:33,901 --> 00:20:36,163
and an ashtray for rule breakers.

562
00:20:36,512 --> 00:20:38,637
The pattern is always the same.

563
00:20:38,817 --> 00:20:41,396
Every strange part on an airplane is

564
00:20:41,396 --> 00:20:44,046
the answer to a question somebody had

565
00:20:44,046 --> 00:20:45,980
to ask, often the hard way.

566
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:49,662
